{"tool_name":"saps_ebi","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"saps_ebi","home":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/jdispatcher/seqstats/saps","summary":"SAPS (Statistical Analysis of Protein Sequences) evaluates a wide variety of protein sequence properties using statistics. Properties considered include compositional biases, clusters and runs of charge and other amino acid types, different kinds and extents of repetitive structures, locally periodic motifs, and anomalous spacings between identical residue types.","addition_date":"2017-12-06T18:14:51Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T13:58:31.814786Z","tool_type":["Web application","Web service"],"version":["1"],"collections":["Job Dispatcher Tools","Proteomics"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:saps_ebi","credit":[{"name":"EMBL-EBI","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]},{"name":"Volker Brendel","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"name":"Job Dispatcher","typeEntity":"Project","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/jdispatcher"}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://brendelgroup.org/bioinformatics2go/SAPS-SSPA.php"},{"type":["General"],"url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/jdispatcher/help"},{"type":["Terms of use"],"url":"http://www.ebi.ac.uk/about/terms-of-use"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["nandana","proteomics.bio.tools"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Sequence composition calculation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0236"}]}],"name":"SAPS (EBI)","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"jdispatcher","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkac240","metadata":{"abstract":"The EMBL-EBI search and sequence analysis tools frameworks provide integrated access to EMBL-EBI's data resources and core bioinformatics analytical tools. EBI Search (https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ebisearch) provides a full-text search engine across nearly 5 billion entries, while the Job Dispatcher tools framework (https://www.ebi.ac.uk/services) enables the scientific community to perform a diverse range of sequence analysis using popular bioinformatics applications. Both allow users to interact through user-friendly web applications, as well as via RESTful and SOAP-based APIs. Here, we describe recent improvements to these services and updates made to accommodate the increasing data requirements during the COVID-19 pandemic.","authors":[{"name":"Basutkar P."},{"name":"Edbali O."},{"name":"Kolesnikov A."},{"name":"Lee J."},{"name":"Lopez R."},{"name":"Madeira F."},{"name":"Madhusoodanan N."},{"name":"Pearce M."},{"name":"Tivey A.R.N."}],"citationCount":1248,"date":"2022-07-05T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"Search and sequence analysis tools services from EMBL-EBI in 2022"},"pmcid":"PMC9252731","pmid":"35412617"},{"doi":"10.1073/pnas.89.6.2002","metadata":{"abstract":"We describe several protein sequence statistics designed to evaluate distinctive attributes of residue content and arrangement in primary structure. Considered are global compositional biases, local clustering of different residue types (e.g., charged residues, Hydrophobic residues, Ser/Thr), long runs of charged or uncharged residues, periodic patterns, counts and distribution of homooligopeptides, and unusual spacings between particular residue types. The computer program SAPS (statistical analysis of protein sequences) calculates all the statistics for any individual protein sequence input and is available for the UNIX environment through electronic mail on request to V.B. (volker@gnomic.stanford.edu).","authors":[{"name":"Blaisdell B.E."},{"name":"Brendel V."},{"name":"Bucher P."},{"name":"Karlin S."},{"name":"Nourbakhsh I.R."}],"citationCount":350,"date":"1992-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","title":"Methods and algorithms for statistical analysis of protein sequences"},"pmcid":"PMC48584","pmid":"1549558","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkae241","metadata":{"abstract":"The EMBL-EBI Job Dispatcher sequence analysis tools framework (https://www.ebi.ac.uk/jdispatcher) enables the scientific community to perform a diverse range of sequence analyses using popular bioinformatics applications. Free access to the tools and required sequence datasets is provided through user-friendly web applications, as well as via RESTful and SOAP-based APIs. These are integrated into popular EMBL-EBI resources such as UniProt, InterPro, ENA and Ensembl Genomes. This paper overviews recent improvements to Job Dispatcher, including its brand new website and documentation, enhanced visualisations, improved job management, and a rising trend of user reliance on the service from low- and middle-income regions.","authors":[{"name":"Butcher S."},{"name":"Eusebi A."},{"name":"Lee J."},{"name":"Lopez R."},{"name":"Madeira F."},{"name":"Madhusoodanan N."},{"name":"Niewielska A."},{"name":"Tivey A.R.N."}],"citationCount":60,"date":"2024-07-05T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"The EMBL-EBI Job Dispatcher sequence analysis tools framework in 2024"},"pmcid":"PMC11223882","pmid":"38597606","type":["Other"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Function analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_1775"},{"term":"Protein structural motifs and surfaces","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0166"},{"term":"Proteomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0121"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"SAPS (EBI)","home":"https://bio.tools/saps_ebi","version":"1","summary":"SAPS (Statistical Analysis of Protein Sequences) evaluates a wide variety of protein sequence properties using statistics. Properties considered include compositional biases, clusters and runs of charge and other amino acid types, different kinds and extents of repetitive structures, locally periodic motifs, and anomalous spacings between identical residue types.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/